WEBVTT

00:00:03.510 --> 00:00:05.310
GUY BOWERMAN: Hello,
I'm Guy Bowerman.

00:00:05.310 --> 00:00:07.890
I'm a Product Manager
for Azure Database

00:00:07.890 --> 00:00:10.230
for Postgres Flexible Server.

00:00:10.230 --> 00:00:12.420
In this Build 2026 session,

00:00:12.420 --> 00:00:16.770
I'll cover migrating workloads
to Postgres on Azure.

00:00:16.770 --> 00:00:18.660
Migration tooling,
it's in a phase

00:00:18.660 --> 00:00:21.180
of rapid improvement right now.

00:00:21.180 --> 00:00:23.280
The technology is
more accessible,

00:00:23.280 --> 00:00:25.980
the cost is lower
than it's ever been.

00:00:25.980 --> 00:00:29.100
I'll be looking at why we're
seeing an increase in migration

00:00:29.100 --> 00:00:33.210
to Postgres, and also why on
Azure, and then I'll be digging

00:00:33.210 --> 00:00:36.450
into the tooling that's
available to help.

00:00:36.450 --> 00:00:39.720
So with that, let's take
a look at the agenda.

00:00:39.720 --> 00:00:42.390
So I'll start with
the why migrate.

00:00:42.390 --> 00:00:46.170
Then I'll take a quick look at
some of the more recent features

00:00:46.170 --> 00:00:51.120
and Build 2026 announcements
for Postgres on Azure.

00:00:51.120 --> 00:00:53.610
And then I'll go through the
tooling that's available

00:00:53.610 --> 00:00:57.150
for migrating Postgres
workloads to Azure.

00:00:57.150 --> 00:01:01.080
And after that, I'll look at how
to migrate Oracle workloads,

00:01:01.080 --> 00:01:02.460
including the schema,

00:01:02.460 --> 00:01:05.490
the database code,
and apps to Azure.

00:01:05.490 --> 00:01:08.810
So with that, let's get started.

00:01:08.810 --> 00:01:11.060
And we'll start with the "why".

00:01:11.060 --> 00:01:13.880
So this industry trend
that we're seeing

00:01:13.880 --> 00:01:19.100
to consolidate database
workloads to Postgres seems

00:01:19.100 --> 00:01:22.590
to be accelerating, and there
are multiple reasons for that.

00:01:22.590 --> 00:01:29.070
I mean, one, Postgres is a
mature database service.

00:01:29.070 --> 00:01:32.580
It's got a highly
optimized query engine.

00:01:32.580 --> 00:01:35.850
The extension model in Postgres
is perfectly suited to adapt

00:01:35.850 --> 00:01:40.590
to any new technologies, whether
it's the latest AI innovations

00:01:40.590 --> 00:01:44.700
or industry-specific
technologies like GIS.

00:01:44.700 --> 00:01:45.930
It's opensource.

00:01:45.930 --> 00:01:49.110
It's backed by an independent
community with no license fee,

00:01:49.110 --> 00:01:50.490
and that's really key.

00:01:50.490 --> 00:01:52.410
Cost is important.

00:01:52.410 --> 00:01:56.370
But what about migration
to Postgres on Azure?

00:01:56.370 --> 00:01:58.630
Let's take a look at that.

00:02:00.270 --> 00:02:04.560
So we take opensource Postgres,
just as is, no changes,

00:02:04.560 --> 00:02:07.110
and run it as a managed
service with all

00:02:07.110 --> 00:02:09.150
of the enterprise
features needed

00:02:09.150 --> 00:02:12.180
to run critical workloads.

00:02:12.180 --> 00:02:13.890
We work with a lot
of household names,

00:02:13.890 --> 00:02:18.420
just kind of showing a few here,
but OpenAI is a good example.

00:02:18.420 --> 00:02:21.150
They're willing to bet
their production workloads

00:02:21.150 --> 00:02:25.080
on managed Postgres
running on Azure.

00:02:25.080 --> 00:02:27.240
One of the reasons for this
is we've been building the

00:02:27.240 --> 00:02:31.650
enterprise features that our
customers are asking for.

00:02:31.650 --> 00:02:34.860
As well as enterprise
fundamentals like performance,

00:02:34.860 --> 00:02:38.910
security, reliability, we're
also paying a lot of attention

00:02:38.910 --> 00:02:41.130
to asks from the
developer community.

00:02:41.130 --> 00:02:44.940
So we'll invest in features
like the VS Code extension,

00:02:44.940 --> 00:02:49.980
the Azure AI extension,
Microsoft Foundry integration.

00:02:49.980 --> 00:02:53.820
But you could still ask, why
not just self-host on a VM?

00:02:53.820 --> 00:02:56.520
You could run a VM in
Azure and host there,

00:02:56.520 --> 00:02:59.280
or host it with Kubernetes.

00:02:59.280 --> 00:03:04.170
So, I really like to think about
the full cost of ownership

00:03:04.170 --> 00:03:06.120
for a production workload.

00:03:06.120 --> 00:03:09.270
Now, there's an
overhead to manage.

00:03:09.270 --> 00:03:12.120
You've got high availability,
disaster recovery,

00:03:12.120 --> 00:03:15.840
you've got scalability,
configuration, integration,

00:03:15.840 --> 00:03:18.360
security, then
there's compliance,

00:03:18.360 --> 00:03:22.440
there's maintenance updates,
major version upgrades.

00:03:22.440 --> 00:03:24.690
I'm not going to read
out every line of this.

00:03:24.690 --> 00:03:26.370
you'll be pleased to know.

00:03:26.370 --> 00:03:27.480
You're welcome to pause.

00:03:27.480 --> 00:03:30.090
But these are just some
of the high-level points,

00:03:30.090 --> 00:03:31.740
and there's a lot more.

00:03:31.740 --> 00:03:35.070
So the key point is, if you're
self-hosting and running

00:03:35.070 --> 00:03:41.130
in production, there's a cost to
staying online and meeting all

00:03:41.130 --> 00:03:43.020
of these requirements.

00:03:43.020 --> 00:03:48.780
So let's just dig into that cost
in another level of detail.

00:03:48.780 --> 00:03:51.540
There was a recent
study by TechTarget

00:03:51.540 --> 00:03:56.550
that measured the before and
after cost of self-managed

00:03:56.550 --> 00:04:00.750
versus running in Azure
Database for Postgres.

00:04:00.750 --> 00:04:04.710
When you're hosting in a managed
environment where you can scale,

00:04:04.710 --> 00:04:07.530
compute, and disk
resources with features

00:04:07.530 --> 00:04:11.220
like built-in connection pooler,
these companies were able

00:04:11.220 --> 00:04:13.440
to not just save costs, but also

00:04:13.440 --> 00:04:16.050
to get better
application performance

00:04:16.050 --> 00:04:18.600
than when they were
running on-premises.

00:04:18.600 --> 00:04:23.970
So, one other thing that I
think is useful to call out is

00:04:23.970 --> 00:04:26.580
when Azure is hosting
an opensource database,

00:04:26.580 --> 00:04:28.350
it's not just about cost.

00:04:28.350 --> 00:04:31.260
We also have a responsibility
to the community,

00:04:31.260 --> 00:04:33.720
to the upstream
opensource community.

00:04:33.720 --> 00:04:36.400
So I'll just touch on
that a little bit.

00:04:37.410 --> 00:04:39.690
We have a team of
Postgres committers.

00:04:39.690 --> 00:04:40.980
Now, they're independent.

00:04:40.980 --> 00:04:42.870
We don't tell them
what to work on.

00:04:42.870 --> 00:04:47.100
They work on contributing to
upstream opensource Postgres.

00:04:47.100 --> 00:04:51.030
If we take Postgres
18, for example,

00:04:51.030 --> 00:04:55.110
some really significant features
for things like query execution,

00:04:55.110 --> 00:04:59.100
async IO, observability,
these were contributed

00:04:59.100 --> 00:05:01.200
by Microsoft committers.

00:05:01.200 --> 00:05:04.650
Now, it's great for Postgres,
but it also means our customers,

00:05:04.650 --> 00:05:08.670
if they run into problems, we
can reach out to the top experts

00:05:08.670 --> 00:05:11.610
in the world on these
topics for help.

00:05:11.610 --> 00:05:13.650
So that's one area
of investment.

00:05:13.650 --> 00:05:15.780
But another that's really
important is we're investing

00:05:15.780 --> 00:05:19.620
in the developer
experience for Postgres.

00:05:19.620 --> 00:05:25.080
Now, the VS Code Postgres
extension, which is free to use,

00:05:25.080 --> 00:05:29.100
it's rapidly becoming a standard
for Postgres developers.

00:05:29.100 --> 00:05:34.380
And it passed a half a million
downloads a few months ago.

00:05:34.380 --> 00:05:36.990
But it's not just a
development environment.

00:05:36.990 --> 00:05:41.100
It's becoming more of a
database operations platform.

00:05:41.100 --> 00:05:44.100
So you can deploy
and manage Postgres.

00:05:44.100 --> 00:05:45.780
You can also do
performance monitoring,

00:05:45.780 --> 00:05:48.420
troubleshooting, visualization.

00:05:48.420 --> 00:05:50.010
There are regular updates

00:05:50.010 --> 00:05:52.440
and there's continuous
improvement on this.

00:05:52.440 --> 00:05:55.470
And I'll come back to this
platform in the context

00:05:55.470 --> 00:05:58.660
of migration in a
few minutes as well.

00:06:00.360 --> 00:06:02.700
So, that's kind of
the why migrate.

00:06:02.700 --> 00:06:04.560
Let's look at some
of the new features

00:06:04.560 --> 00:06:08.160
and announcements
that we've added.

00:06:08.160 --> 00:06:11.400
So, we have a tech
community blog.

00:06:11.400 --> 00:06:13.650
It's called "Microsoft
Blog for Postgres",

00:06:13.650 --> 00:06:17.220
and every month we recap
the latest features

00:06:17.220 --> 00:06:19.140
that have been added.

00:06:19.140 --> 00:06:21.330
The community blog is a
great resource for keeping

00:06:21.330 --> 00:06:23.580
up with our product
and engineering teams,

00:06:23.580 --> 00:06:26.160
and it also really shows
the pace of development,

00:06:26.160 --> 00:06:28.860
because every month we've
got a new set of features

00:06:28.860 --> 00:06:31.290
and we include guidance
on where to look

00:06:31.290 --> 00:06:34.920
up more information
and how to use them.

00:06:34.920 --> 00:06:37.830
I'm going to pick on one area in
particular, which is performance

00:06:37.830 --> 00:06:40.620
and scale, and just take
a quick look at some

00:06:40.620 --> 00:06:44.970
of the latest updates
in that area.

00:06:44.970 --> 00:06:50.280
I'll start with a very recent
announcement, Premium SSD v2.

00:06:50.280 --> 00:06:53.940
It was recently made
generally available.

00:06:53.940 --> 00:07:00.840
It enables up to 80K IOPS, 1.2
GiB per second throughput.

00:07:00.840 --> 00:07:04.440
It's ideal for
IO-intensive workloads.

00:07:04.440 --> 00:07:07.680
What's nice is if
you need more space,

00:07:07.680 --> 00:07:11.460
you can incrementally add it
and only pay for what you need.

00:07:11.460 --> 00:07:15.450
You don't have to double the
size and pay for all of that.

00:07:15.450 --> 00:07:18.420
The graph on the right,
it shows a PG bench test

00:07:18.420 --> 00:07:21.780
across five workload
profiles ranging from 32

00:07:21.780 --> 00:07:25.620
to 256 concurrent clients.

00:07:25.620 --> 00:07:30.690
As the concurrency increases,
you see that SSD v1,

00:07:30.690 --> 00:07:33.570
which maxes out at 20K
IOPS, it starts to max

00:07:33.570 --> 00:07:36.180
out as it reaches these limits.

00:07:36.180 --> 00:07:39.900
While SSD v2, it
continues to scale,

00:07:39.900 --> 00:07:42.540
getting up to four times
the transaction rate

00:07:42.540 --> 00:07:45.120
with sub-millisecond latency.

00:07:45.120 --> 00:07:50.760
Now, there's even more,
a higher IOPS storage

00:07:50.760 --> 00:07:53.730
or a more powerful storage
coming soon called UltraDisk,

00:07:53.730 --> 00:07:58.050
and I'll talk a little bit
about that in the context

00:07:58.050 --> 00:08:01.590
of another new announcement,
which is the Intel

00:08:01.590 --> 00:08:06.000
and AMD v6 compute SKUs are
now generally available.

00:08:06.000 --> 00:08:07.320
So, with these compute SKUs,

00:08:07.320 --> 00:08:13.110
you get up to three times the
memory, double the vCores of v5.

00:08:13.110 --> 00:08:16.410
You also get better
performance per core

00:08:16.410 --> 00:08:18.030
and better price performance.

00:08:18.030 --> 00:08:21.480
So these are based on
the 5th Gen Intel Xeon

00:08:21.480 --> 00:08:25.470
and the 4th Gen AMD EPYC 9004.

00:08:25.470 --> 00:08:32.670
And with these, you will be
able to get up to 400K IOPS

00:08:32.670 --> 00:08:36.330
if you use the v6 SKUs with the
new high-performance UltraDisk

00:08:36.330 --> 00:08:39.450
Storage option
that's coming soon.

00:08:39.450 --> 00:08:42.930
So, these features
enable massive scale-up.

00:08:42.930 --> 00:08:44.640
But then there's
also scaling out

00:08:44.640 --> 00:08:46.980
and parallelization to consider.

00:08:46.980 --> 00:08:49.200
So we'll take a
quick look there.

00:08:49.200 --> 00:08:53.460
We recently made cascading read
replicas generally available.

00:08:53.460 --> 00:08:57.150
So, now Postgres primary can
replicate to read replicas,

00:08:57.150 --> 00:09:00.900
which can in turn replicate to
another layer of read replicas.

00:09:00.900 --> 00:09:04.110
So if you need to scale
out read-heavy workloads,

00:09:04.110 --> 00:09:08.370
you can have up to 30
replicas from one primary.

00:09:08.370 --> 00:09:12.950
It gives you more flexibility
and if you for example want

00:09:12.950 --> 00:09:16.040
to set up cross-region disaster
recovery, you can have some

00:09:16.040 --> 00:09:18.860
of these replicas running
in other regions.

00:09:18.860 --> 00:09:22.100
So this is how customers
like OpenAI, for example,

00:09:22.100 --> 00:09:27.330
are able to have massive
scale in their workloads.

00:09:27.330 --> 00:09:29.730
So that's scaling
out read workloads.

00:09:29.730 --> 00:09:34.230
You can also scale out your
data across multiple nodes.

00:09:34.230 --> 00:09:38.460
So, one feature that it's
been generally available

00:09:38.460 --> 00:09:40.680
for a while now, elastic
cluster, it makes use

00:09:40.680 --> 00:09:43.860
of the Citus Postgres extension.

00:09:43.860 --> 00:09:46.950
Your app can talk to
one Postgres endpoint,

00:09:46.950 --> 00:09:52.500
but you can also shard your data
across multiple Postgres nodes.

00:09:52.500 --> 00:09:55.410
So, this overcomes the
storage and processing limits

00:09:55.410 --> 00:09:57.320
of a standalone server.

00:09:57.320 --> 00:10:00.500
And you can shard by rows
or you can shard by schema,

00:10:00.500 --> 00:10:03.650
depending on whether you want to
make application changes or not,

00:10:03.650 --> 00:10:06.770
and the level of
parallelization that you want.

00:10:06.770 --> 00:10:10.470
So this can lead to massive
performance improvements.

00:10:10.470 --> 00:10:12.660
And this feature, when it
comes to managed Postgres,

00:10:12.660 --> 00:10:14.550
this feature is unique
to Postgres on Azure,

00:10:14.550 --> 00:10:17.670
and it really unlocks scale.

00:10:17.670 --> 00:10:19.350
It's tried and tested, too.

00:10:19.350 --> 00:10:23.580
Behind the scenes, it's powering
other services at Microsoft.

00:10:23.580 --> 00:10:25.710
And in terms of
recent announcements,

00:10:25.710 --> 00:10:27.720
we recently added Postgres 18

00:10:27.720 --> 00:10:31.470
and major version
upgrade support.

00:10:31.470 --> 00:10:33.450
So that's kind of some
of the big performance

00:10:33.450 --> 00:10:35.220
and scale new features.

00:10:35.220 --> 00:10:39.510
What else are we
announcing at Build 2026?

00:10:39.510 --> 00:10:40.830
There's a lot of
new announcements.

00:10:40.830 --> 00:10:42.690
I'll mention a few of them here.

00:10:42.690 --> 00:10:45.270
I've divided them into a few
categories here, "Migrate",

00:10:45.270 --> 00:10:48.390
"Maintain", "Monitor", "Build".

00:10:48.390 --> 00:10:50.460
For "Migrate", I'm
going to go deeper

00:10:50.460 --> 00:10:53.210
into the AI-assisted
migration features.

00:10:53.210 --> 00:10:57.440
Also, Azure Migrate, the Azure
Migrate tool has added Postgres

00:10:57.440 --> 00:10:59.930
server discovery and made
some improvements there.

00:10:59.930 --> 00:11:02.960
So, that really helps with
the first phase of migration,

00:11:02.960 --> 00:11:04.640
which is, what do I have?

00:11:04.640 --> 00:11:08.240
What do I need to migrate?

00:11:08.240 --> 00:11:13.440
Then under "Maintain", a managed
service, it needs maintenance.

00:11:13.440 --> 00:11:16.440
For example, applying minor
version updates to Postgres

00:11:16.440 --> 00:11:21.090
or security patches
to the container OS.

00:11:21.090 --> 00:11:24.510
We've added some features
to increase your control

00:11:24.510 --> 00:11:26.040
over when maintenance
is applied.

00:11:26.040 --> 00:11:28.890
So now you can defer a
maintenance update for up

00:11:28.890 --> 00:11:32.250
to two weeks, and you
can also apply an update

00:11:32.250 --> 00:11:35.310
on demand instead of waiting
for a predefined window.

00:11:35.310 --> 00:11:38.040
So this is really useful
to just get that control

00:11:38.040 --> 00:11:39.270
to apply a maintenance update

00:11:39.270 --> 00:11:42.990
when it doesn't inconvenience
a production workload.

00:11:42.990 --> 00:11:45.450
And then for major
version upgrades,

00:11:45.450 --> 00:11:47.940
we've added a pre-upgrade
validation check.

00:11:47.940 --> 00:11:50.310
We've extended the
pre-upgrade validation check.

00:11:50.310 --> 00:11:52.350
So you can simulate an upgrade,

00:11:52.350 --> 00:11:55.620
fix any issues before
applying an upgrade.

00:11:55.620 --> 00:11:59.970
And the goal here is an upgrade
that works with no surprises.

00:11:59.970 --> 00:12:04.680
Under monitoring, you may have
seen there's a new Grafana

00:12:04.680 --> 00:12:09.000
dashboard option for Postgres
available in the Azure portal.

00:12:09.000 --> 00:12:10.140
These Grafana dashboards,

00:12:10.140 --> 00:12:12.450
they make monitoring your
workload a lot easier

00:12:12.450 --> 00:12:14.550
and customizable.

00:12:14.550 --> 00:12:18.840
Or if you run your analytics and
reporting in Microsoft Fabric,

00:12:18.840 --> 00:12:22.260
then you can mirror your
data from Postgres for free.

00:12:22.260 --> 00:12:26.970
And we've added support for more
scenarios like empty tables,

00:12:26.970 --> 00:12:32.490
more DDL, data definition
language operations,

00:12:32.490 --> 00:12:34.680
to make it more robust.

00:12:34.680 --> 00:12:38.700
And then we've also added
Defender security assessments.

00:12:38.700 --> 00:12:42.660
So you can audit the security
posture for your database.

00:12:42.660 --> 00:12:46.020
For example, does my
database have public access?

00:12:46.020 --> 00:12:48.570
Does it have public schema
access, public role access

00:12:48.570 --> 00:12:50.430
that needs locking down?

00:12:50.430 --> 00:12:53.760
Or does it have the
recommended PG audit settings?

00:12:53.760 --> 00:12:58.050
And then in the category of
building apps, there's a bunch

00:12:58.050 --> 00:13:00.060
of improvements that
have been made to the

00:13:00.060 --> 00:13:01.800
VS Code Postgres extension.

00:13:01.800 --> 00:13:05.310
So an example is query
plan visualization.

00:13:05.310 --> 00:13:07.740
There's an enhanced performance
dashboard, so you can work

00:13:07.740 --> 00:13:12.660
with the query store to
troubleshoot your top queries.

00:13:12.660 --> 00:13:17.190
We've added more operations as
well, like backup and restore.

00:13:17.190 --> 00:13:19.350
I'm going to include a
few links at the end

00:13:19.350 --> 00:13:21.600
to some Build sessions
where you can see some

00:13:21.600 --> 00:13:23.970
of these new features as well.

00:13:23.970 --> 00:13:26.550
And also linking to another area

00:13:26.550 --> 00:13:29.940
which is the high-performance
HorizonDB service that's

00:13:29.940 --> 00:13:31.540
in preview.

00:13:33.660 --> 00:13:35.700
Let's get on to migration then.

00:13:35.700 --> 00:13:38.010
And let's say you have
a Postgres workload.

00:13:38.010 --> 00:13:39.450
Maybe you're running
it on-premises,

00:13:39.450 --> 00:13:41.220
you're running it in a VM.

00:13:41.220 --> 00:13:43.410
Let's take a look at the
tooling that's available

00:13:43.410 --> 00:13:46.890
to move it to Azure.

00:13:46.890 --> 00:13:48.750
Now, whether you're
bringing your workload,

00:13:48.750 --> 00:13:51.090
wherever you're bringing
your workload from,

00:13:51.090 --> 00:13:54.330
we have a migration service
that moves it in one step.

00:13:54.330 --> 00:13:56.700
So, you could be
running in a VM.

00:13:56.700 --> 00:14:00.060
We've also got support for
a bunch of other services

00:14:00.060 --> 00:14:03.540
where you could be
running Postgres.

00:14:03.540 --> 00:14:06.780
In fact, we've also
recently added EDB

00:14:06.780 --> 00:14:09.450
and Huawei as sources as well.

00:14:09.450 --> 00:14:15.120
So with the migration service
in Azure Database for Postgres,

00:14:15.120 --> 00:14:17.160
you can just point
it to the endpoint

00:14:17.160 --> 00:14:19.140
of your source and move it.

00:14:19.140 --> 00:14:22.080
You can migrate online
to minimize downtime,

00:14:22.080 --> 00:14:23.730
or you can do an
offline migration

00:14:23.730 --> 00:14:27.810
that has fewer limitations
and a very easy setup.

00:14:27.810 --> 00:14:29.580
You can also do things
like flexible server

00:14:29.580 --> 00:14:32.550
to flexible server migration.

00:14:32.550 --> 00:14:36.600
It's not officially a
supported option, but it works

00:14:36.600 --> 00:14:39.360
and it gets you random,
it could be useful

00:14:39.360 --> 00:14:41.280
for certain
configuration changes

00:14:41.280 --> 00:14:43.320
that might have
otherwise required a

00:14:43.320 --> 00:14:45.180
point-in-time restore.

00:14:45.180 --> 00:14:46.680
So how do you
actually get to it?

00:14:46.680 --> 00:14:49.650
Let's take a quick look at that.

00:14:49.650 --> 00:14:52.680
You can run the migration
service from the Azure portal

00:14:52.680 --> 00:14:56.160
or on the command
line with Azure CLI.

00:14:56.160 --> 00:14:59.880
If you're using the portal,
the best way to reach it is

00:14:59.880 --> 00:15:01.620
to create a new flexible server

00:15:01.620 --> 00:15:03.990
and then select the
migration option.

00:15:03.990 --> 00:15:07.140
So I've included that in
this little screenshot here.

00:15:07.140 --> 00:15:10.620
From there, you can select your
source and it takes you step

00:15:10.620 --> 00:15:14.190
by step, as well as
listing the prerequisites

00:15:14.190 --> 00:15:16.200
that you need to follow.

00:15:16.200 --> 00:15:21.300
Now, the list of checks there,
worth paying a lot of attention

00:15:21.300 --> 00:15:24.630
to and it also links through
to docs and tutorials

00:15:24.630 --> 00:15:26.850
to help you with these steps.

00:15:26.850 --> 00:15:29.310
If you're using
the command line,

00:15:29.310 --> 00:15:31.860
CLI has full support built in.

00:15:31.860 --> 00:15:35.550
If you do run into limitations,
there are also options

00:15:35.550 --> 00:15:39.720
to use things like the
opensource PG dump and restore.

00:15:39.720 --> 00:15:42.240
I'll include a link to
scenarios where you may want

00:15:42.240 --> 00:15:45.300
to do this as well at the end.

00:15:45.300 --> 00:15:49.140
So that's Postgres-to-Postgres
migration, very straightforward.

00:15:49.140 --> 00:15:51.150
Let's look at something
more complex,

00:15:51.150 --> 00:15:55.080
like migrating Oracle workloads
to Postgres and Azure.

00:15:55.080 --> 00:15:59.040
I touched on some of the
reasons people want to do this.

00:15:59.040 --> 00:16:03.330
Perhaps the number one reason
that we see a big rise in Oracle

00:16:03.330 --> 00:16:07.320
to Postgres migrations
is license cost.

00:16:07.320 --> 00:16:12.720
Now, I'm showing a list price
example here of a license cost

00:16:12.720 --> 00:16:15.900
for the 16-processor license.

00:16:15.900 --> 00:16:17.400
Maybe you'll get a discount.

00:16:17.400 --> 00:16:20.670
But if you compare it to the
Postgres license cost of zero,

00:16:20.670 --> 00:16:25.350
there's a really compelling
reason to look into migration.

00:16:25.350 --> 00:16:28.470
The only caveat which has
stopped a lot of people

00:16:28.470 --> 00:16:30.510
from migrating in the past is

00:16:30.510 --> 00:16:34.980
that cross-database migration
has always been complex

00:16:34.980 --> 00:16:37.800
and therefore costly.

00:16:37.800 --> 00:16:41.250
If you divide the migration
journey into phases,

00:16:41.250 --> 00:16:44.190
it starts with discovery
and assessment.

00:16:44.190 --> 00:16:45.600
But the most challenging,

00:16:45.600 --> 00:16:49.740
complex phases are
really for migration.

00:16:49.740 --> 00:16:52.650
It really migrates in
the database schema,

00:16:52.650 --> 00:16:56.400
migrating the database code,
like store procedures, packages,

00:16:56.400 --> 00:17:00.300
triggers, etc., migrating
the application.

00:17:00.300 --> 00:17:05.280
Maybe you've got a Spring Boot
or like Hibernate app or.NET,

00:17:05.280 --> 00:17:07.350
Python, etc. And
then, of course,

00:17:07.350 --> 00:17:10.470
migrating the data as well.

00:17:10.470 --> 00:17:12.900
So, these four
phases are typically

00:17:12.900 --> 00:17:14.580
where we work with customers on.

00:17:14.580 --> 00:17:17.700
And then of course once you get
your database into Postgres

00:17:17.700 --> 00:17:19.800
and Azure, there's a lot
of performance tuning

00:17:19.800 --> 00:17:21.750
and cloud optimization options

00:17:21.750 --> 00:17:25.040
that you can then
take advantage of.

00:17:25.040 --> 00:17:29.990
So, particularly the first three
steps of these complex areas

00:17:29.990 --> 00:17:33.590
like schema, database code,
and application migration,

00:17:33.590 --> 00:17:35.720
this is where we've
added our own tooling

00:17:35.720 --> 00:17:37.280
to help reduce the complexity

00:17:37.280 --> 00:17:39.960
and lower the cost
of these steps.

00:17:39.960 --> 00:17:41.610
So, what's the tooling?

00:17:41.610 --> 00:17:44.220
So the AI-assisted
Oracle-to-Postgres migration

00:17:44.220 --> 00:17:50.240
tool, it's available now in the
VS Code Postgres extension.

00:17:50.240 --> 00:17:53.450
It takes care of the Oracle
schema, database code,

00:17:53.450 --> 00:17:56.600
and application code
phases of the migration.

00:17:56.600 --> 00:17:59.430
I've included a short link here.

00:17:59.430 --> 00:18:01.890
You can go directly
to more details.

00:18:01.890 --> 00:18:06.780
So with that, let's take
a look into how it works.

00:18:06.780 --> 00:18:09.090
We'll start with the
schema conversion.

00:18:09.090 --> 00:18:13.080
So the migration tool starts by
extracting the Oracle schema

00:18:13.080 --> 00:18:17.460
and then groups it
into related objects.

00:18:17.460 --> 00:18:22.320
It uses an LLM to create related
chunks and then deploys a set

00:18:22.320 --> 00:18:26.780
of schema conversion agents
which interact with the LLM,

00:18:26.780 --> 00:18:29.750
and they create the
SQL code for Postgres.

00:18:29.750 --> 00:18:32.690
They validate the code and
then make further changes

00:18:32.690 --> 00:18:34.650
and iterative improvements.

00:18:34.650 --> 00:18:39.090
So the final schema output
is then available to review,

00:18:39.090 --> 00:18:42.690
and also notes from this phase
are available for the AI tooling

00:18:42.690 --> 00:18:46.080
to use in the code
conversion phases as well.

00:18:46.080 --> 00:18:49.890
So let's take a look
at this in action.

00:18:49.890 --> 00:18:53.700
I'm going to show a schema
migration demo that's recorded

00:18:53.700 --> 00:18:56.250
by Jonathon Frost, who's
one of the program managers

00:18:56.250 --> 00:18:59.640
who worked on this feature.

00:18:59.640 --> 00:19:00.240
JONATHON FROST: Hi there.

00:19:00.240 --> 00:19:03.720
This is Jonathan Frost from
the Azure Postgres team.

00:19:03.720 --> 00:19:06.480
In this demo, I will demonstrate
the Oracle-to-Postgres

00:19:06.480 --> 00:19:08.490
migration experience.

00:19:08.490 --> 00:19:10.530
To begin our migration journey,

00:19:10.530 --> 00:19:13.920
we'll create a new migration
project using the Postgres

00:19:13.920 --> 00:19:17.250
extension and provide a
name for the project.

00:19:17.250 --> 00:19:20.430
Next, we'll connect
to an Oracle server

00:19:20.430 --> 00:19:22.740
by providing
connection parameters.

00:19:22.740 --> 00:19:25.380
In this case, we are
connecting to an instance

00:19:25.380 --> 00:19:28.080
of Oracle version 19c.

00:19:28.080 --> 00:19:31.230
I can list schemas available
on the Oracle server

00:19:31.230 --> 00:19:35.730
and select the one or more I
want to connect to Postgres.

00:19:35.730 --> 00:19:38.880
Now, I'll configure the
scratch Postgres database.

00:19:38.880 --> 00:19:40.230
The scratch database is used

00:19:40.230 --> 00:19:42.480
to test each converted
schema object

00:19:42.480 --> 00:19:45.270
and automatically fix
discovered issues.

00:19:45.270 --> 00:19:48.960
The agentic self-correction
approach using this live

00:19:48.960 --> 00:19:52.380
Postgres database significantly
improves reliability

00:19:52.380 --> 00:19:54.660
of the migration process.

00:19:54.660 --> 00:19:55.380
The last step

00:19:55.380 --> 00:19:57.570
of the configuration
process is connecting

00:19:57.570 --> 00:20:00.480
to an Azure OpenAI endpoint.

00:20:00.480 --> 00:20:03.930
This endpoint is used in
combination with Agent Mode

00:20:03.930 --> 00:20:06.960
to scale migration to
thousands of objects,

00:20:06.960 --> 00:20:10.470
something Copilot Agent Mode
cannot do out of the box.

00:20:10.470 --> 00:20:13.350
Now that we have created a
migration project, I'll go ahead

00:20:13.350 --> 00:20:16.470
and click the "Migrate" button
to start the extraction

00:20:16.470 --> 00:20:18.270
and conversion process.

00:20:18.270 --> 00:20:21.870
The first stage is
extracting the Oracle DDL,

00:20:21.870 --> 00:20:25.200
which is indicated by
this top progress bar.

00:20:25.200 --> 00:20:28.320
The second stage is
converting each chunk of DDL

00:20:28.320 --> 00:20:31.290
by the multiagent
orchestration workflow.

00:20:31.290 --> 00:20:33.930
At this point, the chunk was
attempted to be compiled

00:20:33.930 --> 00:20:35.940
against the Postgres database.

00:20:35.940 --> 00:20:39.750
And at this point, some errors
occurred in the compilation.

00:20:39.750 --> 00:20:42.870
In the following step, the
conversion agent took this

00:20:42.870 --> 00:20:46.350
into account and reconverted
this chunk of DDL

00:20:46.350 --> 00:20:49.470
and recompiled it against the
Postgres scratch database.

00:20:49.470 --> 00:20:53.610
Lastly, the status updates show
the compilation was successful

00:20:53.610 --> 00:20:56.640
and the chunk was applied
to the Postgres database.

00:20:56.640 --> 00:21:00.240
Additionally, we can see two
coding notes were captured.

00:21:00.240 --> 00:21:03.270
Coding notes are context
items that may be helpful

00:21:03.270 --> 00:21:05.160
to an application migration,

00:21:05.160 --> 00:21:08.490
and our additional application
conversion tooling brings these

00:21:08.490 --> 00:21:09.810
into the process.

00:21:09.810 --> 00:21:13.590
Here, we see a comprehensive
migration report is generated

00:21:13.590 --> 00:21:16.890
and shows the details of the
number of objects converted,

00:21:16.890 --> 00:21:20.940
object types, and the Postgres
extensions detected and used

00:21:20.940 --> 00:21:23.300
for the conversion context.

00:21:23.300 --> 00:21:26.720
This test conversion only
processed 34 objects.

00:21:26.720 --> 00:21:29.120
We have had customers
successfully test this

00:21:29.120 --> 00:21:32.160
on a scale of over
2,000 objects.

00:21:32.160 --> 00:21:34.260
Now that everything
has been converted,

00:21:34.260 --> 00:21:37.320
we can use our Postgres
extension visualize schema

00:21:37.320 --> 00:21:40.890
feature to quickly validate the
tables and relationships now

00:21:40.890 --> 00:21:42.390
in the Postgres database.

00:21:42.390 --> 00:21:43.500
This looks good.

00:21:43.500 --> 00:21:46.410
Next, let's review
the converted DDL.

00:21:46.410 --> 00:21:49.440
To do this, let's right-click
on the addresses table

00:21:49.440 --> 00:21:51.540
and compare the Oracle version

00:21:51.540 --> 00:21:54.930
and the generated Postgres
version side-by-side.

00:21:54.930 --> 00:21:56.520
All right, this also looks good.

00:21:56.520 --> 00:22:00.150
Now, let's go back to the
migration project wizard.

00:22:00.150 --> 00:22:02.190
After this initial
conversion is done,

00:22:02.190 --> 00:22:04.380
we can enter interactive mode.

00:22:04.380 --> 00:22:06.480
For every conversion
issue that was discovered

00:22:06.480 --> 00:22:08.610
and couldn't be
resolved automatically,

00:22:08.610 --> 00:22:12.450
the tool has created a follow-up
task for the developer.

00:22:12.450 --> 00:22:14.700
You can see here in this table,

00:22:14.700 --> 00:22:16.560
we have a handful
of tasks to review.

00:22:16.560 --> 00:22:19.320
Let's work on this complex
trigger one together.

00:22:19.320 --> 00:22:21.090
I will click the
"Run Task" button

00:22:21.090 --> 00:22:22.950
to help me with this task.

00:22:22.950 --> 00:22:26.010
The migration tool has generated
a custom prompt for me

00:22:26.010 --> 00:22:29.280
that gives all the context to
the agent mode and enables it

00:22:29.280 --> 00:22:31.770
to suggest a solution
for the issue.

00:22:31.770 --> 00:22:34.170
Agent mode was able to
recognize the issue

00:22:34.170 --> 00:22:36.150
and provide a correction.

00:22:36.150 --> 00:22:38.970
Let's have a look at the two
files that were modified.

00:22:38.970 --> 00:22:40.260
These look good.

00:22:40.260 --> 00:22:43.650
We can keep the changes and
finalize the migration.

00:22:43.650 --> 00:22:47.010
In this demo, we were able to
connect to an Oracle database

00:22:47.010 --> 00:22:50.130
and convert its schema
into the Postgres format.

00:22:50.130 --> 00:22:53.100
We were able to perform it
at scale, and then track

00:22:53.100 --> 00:22:57.090
and resolve complex migration
issues in interactive mode.

00:22:57.090 --> 00:22:59.220
GUY BOWERMAN: So, that's
schema conversion.

00:22:59.220 --> 00:23:03.210
Now, for application conversion,
you'll be creating a

00:23:03.210 --> 00:23:06.300
VS Code workspace
migration project

00:23:06.300 --> 00:23:09.900
and copy the application
files locally.

00:23:09.900 --> 00:23:13.980
The VS Code extension leverages
GitHub Copilot Agent Mode.

00:23:13.980 --> 00:23:17.700
It uses a Claude model with
custom conversion blueprints

00:23:17.700 --> 00:23:21.210
and report templates from
the schema conversion phase.

00:23:21.210 --> 00:23:24.900
The agents are using LLMTs,

00:23:24.900 --> 00:23:27.780
or large language model-based
translation layers,

00:23:27.780 --> 00:23:31.590
to convert the code queries
and application logic

00:23:31.590 --> 00:23:34.080
across the technology stacks.

00:23:34.080 --> 00:23:36.780
It also uses an Azure database
for Postgres instance

00:23:36.780 --> 00:23:41.320
to validate versions and
extensions as it goes along.

00:23:42.810 --> 00:23:45.990
With that, let's take a
look at that in action.

